    [other] context-not-an-accountable-requirement/context-block-shares-message-with-requirements
      The separation of context from the requirement list depends on `assemble` placing TASK_CONTEXT (35) in a different message from SUBJECT (40). If `assemble` groups by kind range or merges adjacent blocks, the context ends up in the same message as the requirements, so the model may read it as one more listed requirement.
      src/acceptance/request_blocks.py#0
      src/acceptance/requirement/obligations.py#4
      pairs: 2 put to the model, 0 skipped once the defect was covered
    [other] context-not-an-accountable-requirement/system-prompt-still-demands-accounting-for-all-content
      The unchanged `_SYSTEM_PROMPT` may still instruct the model to account for everything in the prompt, or to derive obligations from everything it is shown. That would contradict the context label and push the model toward obligations or dispositions for context content.
      src/acceptance/requirement/obligations.py#2
      src/acceptance/requirement/obligations.py#4
      pairs: 4 put to the model, 126 skipped once the defect was covered
    [missing_case] context-not-an-accountable-requirement/context-section-parse-leaks-into-mandate
      The parser's Context handling can misroute content. A same-level or shallower heading that is not a mandate section, such as `## Notes`, resets `section` to a non-mandate name, so its blocks go to `unclaimed` rather than context. A deeper heading naming a mandate section ends Context. Context text under variant headings (for example 'Background') is not recognised as context, so it falls under `unclaimed`, or under `# Task` and becomes a requirement.
